      "headline": "The Three Muscles of Staying on Track",
      "keyInsight": "We love to believe we can multi-task, but science shows it is an absolute biological myth. Your brain does not do two hard things at once. Instead, it plays a frantic game of ping-pong. It rapidly jumps back and forth between two different tasks, bleeding energy at every bounce. What lets us aim for high goals in the first place is a special toolkit called cognitive control. It has three parts: attention, working memory, and goal management. Attention points the spotlight. Working memory holds the fragile blueprint in your head. Goal management decides which step comes next. The twist is that while our ambition has no ceiling, this toolkit has a tiny battery. When you try to cook dinner and take a work call, you are not being productive. You are simply forcing a brilliant engine to sputter and stall.",
      "content": "Picture driving down a busy highway in pouring rain. You must keep your eyes on the road, remember your exit number, and ignore billboard advertisements. That requires pure mental control. Without it, you would simply drift toward whatever shiny sign caught your eye.\n\nYour mind relies on three main tools to get you across the finish line. First is selective attention. It acts like a bouncer at a club door, letting in useful details and rejecting background gossip. Second is working memory. Think of it as a small whiteboard on your desk where you jot down a phone number before dialing. It holds information for only a few seconds before wiping clean.\n\nThird is goal switching. This is your mental traffic controller directing cars at an intersection. When you switch from writing a report to checking a spreadsheet, the controller must stop one lane and wave the other forward. Every single switch causes a traffic jam. That friction slows you down and invites silly mistakes.",
