      "chapterNumber": 9,
      "headline": "The Restless Hunter Inside Your Head",
      "keyInsight": "The most shocking discovery in modern focus science is that outside alerts are only half the problem. More than half the time, nobody interrupts you; you interrupt yourself! You are sitting alone in total silence, and out of nowhere, you grab your phone. Why do we do this? Because our ancient ancestors were nomadic foragers whose survival depended on scanning the forest for scattered berry patches. Today, your brain treats information exactly like food. When your current work task becomes a little tough, boring, or slow, your mental foraging meter drops. An internal alarm bell rings, sparking a wave of anxiety. To calm that itch, your ancient foraging instinct drives you to hunt for fresh informational berries on another browser tab. You trade high-value deep work for low-value mental sugar, trapped in a loop of your own restlessness.",
      "content": "Imagine a hungry squirrel digging in a dry patch of dirt. If acorns are plentiful, the squirrel stays put and keeps digging. But if digging gets hard and yields nothing for three minutes, an ancient instinct kicks in. The squirrel abandons the spot and scurries to the next tree. Biologists call this the optimal foraging theory. Animals constantly calculate whether the energy spent searching a spot is worth the calories found. The moment the current patch looks barren, internal tension compels the animal to move.\n\nHumans apply this exact same math to information. In prehistoric times, spotting a fresh track or a distant berry bush kept you alive. Your brain rewarded you with a surge of pleasure whenever you uncovered something new. Fast forward to your office desk. You are tackling a demanding spreadsheet or writing a complex proposal. Your progress is slow, effortful, and heavy. Suddenly, your brain registers the current spot as dry and unrewarding. It feels an uncomfortable pinch of boredom and restlessness.\n\nInstead of enduring the discomfort to break through, you reach out and click a news feed. Instant reward! A funny video, a juicy headline, a social message. Your brain gets its informational calorie hit with zero physical effort. The tragic irony is that modern technology has placed thousands of lush berry bushes just an inch away from your fingers. We do not need an external beep to pull us away. Our inner forager abandons the hard work on its own, forever hunting for the next quick bite.",
      "keyTakeaway": "We interrupt ourselves because our ancient foraging instinct hates boredom and constantly hunts for fresh information.",
      "audioSnippetText": "More than half of our daily interruptions come from inside our own heads. When work gets difficult, our restless brain scours the digital jungle for quick informational snacks.",
